BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Finally Seen: From Diagnosis to Daily Life
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20260507T183000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20260507T203000
UID:blogto-233706@blogto.com
ATTACH;FMTTYPE=image/jpeg:https://blogto-production2-baselayer-display.blo
 gto.com/events/2026/04/15/original_image2ce9cde7-c1a7-4983-bc3d-33db0da460
 f6.jpg
DESCRIPTION:A conversation on mental health and neurodivergence in women 
 — and what it means to truly be seen.\nFinally Seen: From Diagnosis to D
 aily Life is a powerful and affirming panel experience exploring how neuro
 divergence and mental health intersect in women across the lifespan — in
  healthcare\, relationships\, mental health\, and everyday life.\nCreated 
 in collaboration with womenmind\, Women’s Health Research Cluster\, CAMH
  Azrieli Adult Neurodevelopmental Centre\, and Science Up First\, this eve
 nt moves beyond the typical conversation around “late diagnosis” to ex
 plore something deeper: what it means to be recognized\, understood\, and 
 supported.\nThrough a conversation featuring lived experience\, clinical i
 nsight\, and research\, we’ll explore:\n· The latest research into wome
 n and neurodivergence\, including dispelling common myths.\n· From “lat
 e diagnosis” to being seen: diagnostic delay\, masking\, and access to c
 are.\n· Mental health & identity: burnout\, anxiety\, depression\, and mi
 xed emotions after recognition.\n·Respectful healthcare: autonomy\, commu
 nication needs\, and sensory-aware environments.\n· Sexual & reproductive
  health: gaps in neuro-affirming care across the lifespan.\n· Designing b
 etter systems: shifting from “fixing individuals” to changing environm
 ents.\nWhat to Expect\nThis is more than a panel — it’s an experience.
 \nA thoughtfully moderated panel discussion\nLive audience Q&A\nInteractiv
 e activities and reflection opportunities\nResource and information statio
 ns\nLight refreshments\nEverything is intentionally designed with accessib
 ility and neurodivergent experiences in mind.\nWho Should Attend\nThis eve
 nt is open to:\nNeurodivergent women and gender-diverse people\nAllies: pa
 rtners\, caregivers\, friends\, family\nHealthcare providers\, educators\,
  and professionals\nAnyone looking to better understand and support neurod
 ivergent people\n“Women” refers to a broad and inclusive gender identi
 ty and includes anyone who identifies as a woman.\nWhat You’ll Walk Away
  With\nPractical tools to recognize and respond to burnout\nLanguage for s
 elf-advocacy in healthcare and mental health settings\nSensory and communi
 cation strategies you can use right away\nA deeper understanding of identi
 ty\, masking\, and support needs\nTangible ways to show up as a better all
 y
LOCATION:Arrell Family Foundation Auditorium at the Centre For Addiction a
 nd Mental Health
URL:https://www.blogto.com/events/finally-seen-from-diagnosis-to-daily-lif
 e-toronto/
X-SOURCE:blogTO
END:VEVENT
