BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Discovering Romanian Cuisine by Maria Colonescu
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20260509T120000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20260509T143000
UID:blogto-234284@blogto.com
ATTACH;FMTTYPE=image/jpeg:https://blogto-production2-baselayer-display.blo
 gto.com/events/2026/04/28/original_image9db11ed1-2f48-43d0-a53f-a93f3664fa
 a4.jpg
DESCRIPTION:Romanian cuisine is rarely seen in Toronto's food scene\, but 
 well-loved by those who come to know it. A mix of Eastern European\, Balka
 n\, Roma and Ottoman influences\, Romanian food has many familiar elements
  but still holds many unexpected surprises. Performer and cook Maria Colon
 escu is excited to share some of Romania's rich flavours and history\, wit
 h a spotlight on three delicious vegetarian dishes tucked among what can b
 e a very meat-centric culinary tradition.\n-----\nCiorbă falsă de burtă
 \nThis is a vegetarian take on the traditional Romanian tripe soup\, an in
 tensely flavoured but refreshing dish\, said to have been a favourite hang
 over remedy in olden times. Root vegetables and oyster mushrooms in a crea
 my broth of sour cream and egg yolks are freshened up with garlic and vine
 gar.\nMămăligă cu branză.\nThe quintessential peasant's meal\, warm co
 mfort after a hard day in a cold field. Plain and delicious - this simple 
 dish is a combination of boiled cornmeal\, cheese\, sour cream\, eggs\, an
 d - of course - garlic sauce. Sometimes combined with sausages or other me
 at\, but perfectly satisfying on its own.\nBudincă\nA dessert specific to
  Transylvania\, this is another plain but rich dish\, often had for breakf
 ast and meant to keep you going. Maria describes it as dessert pasta\, to 
 the confusion of all. As that name implies\, the base mixture is pasta and
  sweet cheese\, finished off with rum flavour\, much loved and used in Rom
 anian sweet recipes.\n-----\nMaria Colonescu is a theatre artist and kitch
 en enthusiast\, passionate about teaching what she can and sharing her cul
 ture as a Romanian immigrant. As a long-time vegetarian raised with meat-h
 eavy traditions\, Maria has also spent years experimenting with vegetarian
  cooking in order to keep the tastes and traditions of home alive but in h
 armony with her environmental values. She recently presented an immersive 
 theatre experience in the form of a cooking class\, addressing the immigra
 nt experience and loss of tradition and of self\, through the lens of food
 .\naequiluxproductions.ca | @colonescu.maria
LOCATION:The Depanneur
URL:https://www.blogto.com/events/discovering-romanian-cuisine-by-maria-co
 lonescu-toronto-2/
X-SOURCE:blogTO
END:VEVENT
