BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:bol\, brown boy\, bol
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20260520T193000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20260521T170000
UID:blogto-230967@blogto.com
ATTACH;FMTTYPE=image/jpeg:https://blogto-production2-baselayer-display.blo
 gto.com/events/2026/02/15/original_imagef0a4da97-3804-4543-84d0-ed64718d95
 92.jpg
DESCRIPTION:bol\, brown boy\, bol is a cheeky\, heartfelt solo show about 
 Nawaaz's journey of reclaiming his voice and cultural inheritance through 
 learning tabla\, a northern Indian percussion instrument.\nTwenty years of
  being a “coconut” has caught up to Nawaaz\, who’s crashed into a du
 al crisis of identity and faith. Divine intervention sends him a musical G
 uru\, who guides him on a journey towards self-acceptance and love. Direct
 ed by Tiffany Wu\, bol\, brown boy\, bol offers a rich musical landscape m
 erging Indian classical music with 90s Bollywood nostalgia\, grunge\, rap\
 , and hip hop. Weaving storytelling with the rhythms of tabla\, creator an
 d performer Nawaaz speaks up about the impact of Canadian racism on his id
 entity and shares his story of finding his way back to himself.
LOCATION:Native Earth's Aki Studio
URL:https://www.blogto.com/events/bol-brown-boy-bol-toronto/
X-SOURCE:blogTO
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Bridging Minds: Health & Science Conversations
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20260520T180000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20260520T190000
UID:blogto-234009@blogto.com
ATTACH;FMTTYPE=image/jpeg:https://blogto-production2-baselayer-display.blo
 gto.com/events/2026/04/22/original_image84419bd2-a1c4-4ffa-8081-e8fd5cba47
 04.jpg
DESCRIPTION:1 in 6 people globally are now affected by loneliness\, with s
 ignificant impacts on their health and well-being. Whereas social connecti
 on can protect health across the lifespan – reducing inflammation\, impr
 oving mental health and lowering the risk of serious health problems. Wher
 e we live can also play a big role in helping us feel more connected. Join
  us online on May 20 to learn more about loneliness\, its impact on health
 \, and to understand how communities\, especially naturally occurring reti
 rement communities (NORCs)\, can support stronger relationships and health
 ier aging.\n\nAbout the Series:\nBridging Minds: Health & Science Conversa
 tions is a partnership between the Toronto Public Library and Women’s Co
 llege Hospital. Through monthly discussions led by experts from across cli
 nical areas and scientific disciplines\, this series provides attendees wi
 th the opportunity to engage directly with our topics and speakers and emp
 owers communities to navigate health and science topics with confidence\, 
 inspiring a future of informed decision-making.\nReserve Your Spot Today!\
 nThis virtual event is free to join\, but registration is required. Once r
 egistered\, you’ll receive an email with details on how to join the sess
 ion.\nWe can’t wait to see you there!
LOCATION:Virtual Event
URL:https://www.blogto.com/events/bridging-minds-health-science-conversati
 ons-toronto/
X-SOURCE:blogTO
END:VEVENT
