BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Third Spaces Event: Toronto Trivia Night Edition
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20260528T180000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20260528T210000
UID:blogto-234508@blogto.com
ATTACH;FMTTYPE=image/jpeg:https://blogto-production2-baselayer-display.blo
 gto.com/events/2026/05/01/original_image57b2ac11-de61-4c07-b247-555ed36099
 b1.jpg
DESCRIPTION:Whether you’re a Toronto trivia nerd or just here to guess t
 he memes\, there’s something for everyone — plus tea\, snacks and brag
 ging rights.\nWant to flex your knowledge of our city? Or just see how man
 y famous Torontonian hits you can guess? Then\, sign up for The Green Line
 ’s upcoming third spaces event.\nMark your calendars\, and join us every
  third or fourth Thursday of the month for low-key gatherings hosted by ou
 r community engagement producer Adele Lukusa.\nCome on down to Youngplace 
 at 180 Shaw St.\, Suite 314 (across from Trinity Bellwoods Park in West Qu
 een West) on Thursday\, May 28\, from 6 to 9 p.m. Then put on your best ga
 me face\, as we look to crown Toronto’s Next Top Municipal Menace — pr
 izes included! Compete by yourself or in a team with friends old and new. 
 We’ll be covering as much ground as we can\, from TTC history to iconic 
 memes\, so get to studying.\nSupplies\, tea and snacks are provided with y
 our ticket\, which costs $15 (there's a limited time offer of 2 for 1 tick
 ets so you can bring a friend for free) — so all that’s missing is you
 . Get tickets at THEGREENLINE.TO/events or here: https://www.eventbrite.ca
 /e/third-spaces-event-toronto-trivia-night-edition-tickets-1988527281629\n
 See you there!
LOCATION:Youngplace
URL:https://www.blogto.com/events/third-spaces-event-toronto-trivia-night-
 edition-toronto/
X-SOURCE:blogTO
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Transit and Towers: Transit-Oriented Development in Toronto
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20260528T173000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20260528T193000
UID:blogto-234279@blogto.com
ATTACH;FMTTYPE=image/jpeg:https://blogto-production2-baselayer-display.blo
 gto.com/events/2026/04/28/original_image8247a8ca-fae7-4073-a166-6d440332b6
 a6.jpg
DESCRIPTION:Toronto is one of North America’s great transit cities\, and
  nowhere is that clearer than along the Yonge Street corridor. This is a s
 tretch where every subway stop has a development story attached to it and 
 where the relationship between transit and neighbourhood infrastructure ha
 s been playing out for over 70 years.\nThis two-hour walking tour\, suppor
 ted by UrbanToronto\, traces that story from Union Station to Eglinton Sta
 tion\, stopping at five sites that together explain how transit shapes cit
 ies: where density comes from\, why some buildings get torn down and other
 s don’t\, what happens when ambitious projects run short on cash\, and w
 hy cranes appear in a neighbourhood years before transit options catch up.
 \nAlong the way you’ll see Canada’s first supertall skyscraper mid-con
 struction\, a 1930 Art Deco interior that survived a department store’s 
 collapse and is finally getting the tower it was always meant to have\, an
 d a 47-storey tower whose design challenges everything typical about how T
 oronto builds near transit.\nNo architecture background required is requir
 ed! Just curiosity about the city and how transit catalyzes development.
LOCATION:Union Station Great Hall
URL:https://www.blogto.com/events/transit-and-towers-transit-oriented-deve
 lopment-in-toronto-toronto/
X-SOURCE:blogTO
END:VEVENT
